{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-blog-post-js","path":"/AD-Breaching/","result":{"data":{"markdownRemark":{"html":"<h1>What is Active Directory?</h1>\n<p>Active Directory is a service launched by Windows Server 2000, with its main focus of authentication and authorization to users on the network.</p>\n<p>Read More about it <a href=\"https://activedirectorypro.com/what-is-active-directory/\">here</a></p>\n<h1>Try Hack Me Room</h1>\n<p>In this blog we will walk through and understand <a href=\"https://tryhackme.com/room/introductiontoactivedirectorybreaching\">Intro to AD Breaching</a></p>\n<h2>Task 1: Introduction</h2>\n<p>This task introduces that the first set of valid credentials is required to begin enumeration or move laterally or escalate privileges. Process of obtaining those initial credentials is called <em>breaching</em>.</p>\n<p>Let's connect to the network first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Method 1: AttackBox. Simply click on the \"Start AttackBox\" button at the top.</li>\n<li>Method 2: OpenVPN connection. Connect to the network using the VPN profile file created.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The machines are connected to the network via VPN in either of the case. To ensure name resolution we perform the following command <code class=\"language-text\">sudo nano /etc/hosts</code>.\nAnd then we add the following:</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"text\"><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">192.168.12.100  thm.loc \n192.168.12.71   git.thm.loc ci.thm.loc printer.thm.loc \n192.168.12.51   SERVER1.thm.loc</code></pre></div>\n<p>Once this is done. Our AttackBox is ready to breach into the Active Directory.</p>\n<h2>Task 2: Active Directory Breaches</h2>\n<p>This task talks about what is Active Directory Breaching and the AD attack surface.</p>\n<p>A typical AD environment exposes a range of target-able services and protocols during the breaching phase.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>SMB(TCP 445) : File sharing, printers and remote administration.</li>\n<li>LDAP(TCP 389/636) : Misconfigured devices often store LDAP credentials.</li>\n<li>HTTP/HTTPS : Web-based services.</li>\n<li>Kerberos(TCP/UDP 88) : Domain's primary authentication protocol.</li>\n<li>DNS(TCP/UDP 53) : Used to resolve hostnames within AD environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Task 3: OSINT and Target Reconnaissance</h2>\n<p>In this task we move towards finding valid usernames. We do so by enumerating usernames through Kerberos, as it does not trigger account lockouts. A wordlist is saved into /root/usernames.txt, as given by the THM.</p>\n<p>The wordlist can also be created manually using various techniques and sources.</p>\n<p><em>Step 1</em>: Install Kerbrute\nGo to <code class=\"language-text\">https://github.com/ropnop/kerbrute/releases/tag/v1.0.3</code> and install the appropriate version</p>\n<p>Go to Downloads and make the module executable. <code class=\"language-text\">chmod +x kerbrute_linux_amd64</code>\nand finally for easy usage move it using <code class=\"language-text\">mv kerbrute_linux_amd64 kerbrute</code></p>\n<p><em>Step 2</em>: Enumerate the wordlist on the AD domain controller.\n<code class=\"language-text\">kerbrute userenum -d thm.loc --dc 192.168.12.100 \\root\\usernames.txt</code></p>\n<p>It displays all the valid usernames.\n<span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 564px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/2dfc1c9360f4e8c20faa4130ff93dab7/dfbb8/ValidUsernames.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 141.66666666666666%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        alt=\"Valid usernames\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/2dfc1c9360f4e8c20faa4130ff93dab7/dfbb8/ValidUsernames.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/2dfc1c9360f4e8c20faa4130ff93dab7/4abca/ValidUsernames.png 180w,\n/static/2dfc1c9360f4e8c20faa4130ff93dab7/65e49/ValidUsernames.png 360w,\n/static/2dfc1c9360f4e8c20faa4130ff93dab7/dfbb8/ValidUsernames.png 564w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 564px) 100vw, 564px\"\n        style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n        loading=\"lazy\"\n        decoding=\"async\"\n      />\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<h2>Task 4: Credential Discovery</h2>\n<p>Task talks about several places that where developers and administrators accidentally, such as Git Repositories, CI/CD platforms and file shares.</p>\n<h3>Hunting Credential in Git Repositories</h3>\n<p>The task has provided us with an exposed <a href=\"http://git.thm.loc/megacorp-admin/webapp-deploy\">Github Repository</a>\nHowever, in real scenarios, we have to find the exposed repositories by simply searching companies name in Github.</p>\n<p>On finding the repositories we use <code class=\"language-text\">git log</code> to look for password/credentials.</p>\n<p><span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 720px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/319c958927221b38f30caad04928d76e/1e677/GithubCredential.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 56.111111111111114%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        alt=\"Github Credential Hunting\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/319c958927221b38f30caad04928d76e/1efcc/GithubCredential.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/319c958927221b38f30caad04928d76e/4abca/GithubCredential.png 180w,\n/static/319c958927221b38f30caad04928d76e/65e49/GithubCredential.png 360w,\n/static/319c958927221b38f30caad04928d76e/1efcc/GithubCredential.png 720w,\n/static/319c958927221b38f30caad04928d76e/ff6c2/GithubCredential.png 1080w,\n/static/319c958927221b38f30caad04928d76e/1e677/GithubCredential.png 1195w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\"\n        style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n        loading=\"lazy\"\n        decoding=\"async\"\n      />\n  </a>\n    </span>\nJen5k1ns2025! is the retrieved password.\nmc-webapp-s3cret-k3y is the secret key.\nMegaCorp01! is the default password.</p>\n<h3>Hunting Credentials in Jenkins</h3>\n<p>Jenkins is a common CI/CD platform on internal network, where credentials are often left.</p>\n<p>Hunting through the Jenkins web interface we further find the default password in console output\n<span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 720px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/85354862068470bdb27a21e91c770149/bf94d/JenkinsCredential.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 55.55555555555556%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        alt=\"Jenkins Credential\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/85354862068470bdb27a21e91c770149/1efcc/JenkinsCredential.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/85354862068470bdb27a21e91c770149/4abca/JenkinsCredential.png 180w,\n/static/85354862068470bdb27a21e91c770149/65e49/JenkinsCredential.png 360w,\n/static/85354862068470bdb27a21e91c770149/1efcc/JenkinsCredential.png 720w,\n/static/85354862068470bdb27a21e91c770149/ff6c2/JenkinsCredential.png 1080w,\n/static/85354862068470bdb27a21e91c770149/bf94d/JenkinsCredential.png 1351w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\"\n        style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n        loading=\"lazy\"\n        decoding=\"async\"\n      />\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<h2>Task 5: Password Spraying</h2>\n<p>In this approach we try a single password against many accounts.</p>\n<p>NetExec tool is used to perform spraying of the passwords we retrieved in the previous task on the valid usernames we retrieved in Task 3.</p>\n<p><span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 720px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/9801042340d4fb31a5ee58b9cd77b541/645d7/ValidCredentialPair.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 53.888888888888886%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        alt=\"Valid Pair\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/9801042340d4fb31a5ee58b9cd77b541/1efcc/ValidCredentialPair.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/9801042340d4fb31a5ee58b9cd77b541/4abca/ValidCredentialPair.png 180w,\n/static/9801042340d4fb31a5ee58b9cd77b541/65e49/ValidCredentialPair.png 360w,\n/static/9801042340d4fb31a5ee58b9cd77b541/1efcc/ValidCredentialPair.png 720w,\n/static/9801042340d4fb31a5ee58b9cd77b541/ff6c2/ValidCredentialPair.png 1080w,\n/static/9801042340d4fb31a5ee58b9cd77b541/645d7/ValidCredentialPair.png 1260w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\"\n        style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n        loading=\"lazy\"\n        decoding=\"async\"\n      />\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p>The above shows that 'MegaCorp01!' is the password for accounts 'alice.moore' and 'dev.intern', i.e.; Alice Moore and Developer Intern, has kept the default password. Hence, we have attained a valid credential pair.</p>\n<h2>Task 6: Coercion Attack</h2>\n<p>Coercion tricks a device or user into sending authentication material to an attacker-controlled listener.</p>\n<p>In this we experience two techniques LDAP passback attack and a file based Coercion.</p>\n<h3>LDAP Passback Attack.</h3>\n<p>Network devices like printer, scanners and multifunction peripherals integrate with Active Directory via LDAP. For this to work, the device stores a set of LDAP credential.</p>\n<p><em>Step 1</em>: Go to the printer application's web admin portal and login using admin:admin\n<span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 720px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/0239b29b81e393971444130639194b86/c0265/PrinterWebPage.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 89.99999999999999%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        alt=\"Printer WebPage\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/0239b29b81e393971444130639194b86/1efcc/PrinterWebPage.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/0239b29b81e393971444130639194b86/4abca/PrinterWebPage.png 180w,\n/static/0239b29b81e393971444130639194b86/65e49/PrinterWebPage.png 360w,\n/static/0239b29b81e393971444130639194b86/1efcc/PrinterWebPage.png 720w,\n/static/0239b29b81e393971444130639194b86/c0265/PrinterWebPage.png 908w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\"\n        style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n        loading=\"lazy\"\n        decoding=\"async\"\n      />\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p><em>Step 2</em>: Go to LDAP settings and set Server Address as lan0 of attackbox and LDAP port as 3489\nAnd save changes\n<span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 720px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/d3fce66d328083050251a098bb508b2f/c0265/LDAPConf.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 89.99999999999999%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        alt=\"LDAP Config\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/d3fce66d328083050251a098bb508b2f/1efcc/LDAPConf.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/d3fce66d328083050251a098bb508b2f/4abca/LDAPConf.png 180w,\n/static/d3fce66d328083050251a098bb508b2f/65e49/LDAPConf.png 360w,\n/static/d3fce66d328083050251a098bb508b2f/1efcc/LDAPConf.png 720w,\n/static/d3fce66d328083050251a098bb508b2f/c0265/LDAPConf.png 908w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\"\n        style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n        loading=\"lazy\"\n        decoding=\"async\"\n      />\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p><em>Step 3</em> Choose test connection and run <code class=\"language-text\">nc -lvnp 348</code>\n<span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 720px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/f1b285b70c3a38399440f1f3ca803207/c0265/LDAPPassword.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 41.11111111111111%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        alt=\"LDAP Password\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/f1b285b70c3a38399440f1f3ca803207/1efcc/LDAPPassword.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/f1b285b70c3a38399440f1f3ca803207/4abca/LDAPPassword.png 180w,\n/static/f1b285b70c3a38399440f1f3ca803207/65e49/LDAPPassword.png 360w,\n/static/f1b285b70c3a38399440f1f3ca803207/1efcc/LDAPPassword.png 720w,\n/static/f1b285b70c3a38399440f1f3ca803207/c0265/LDAPPassword.png 908w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\"\n        style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n        loading=\"lazy\"\n        decoding=\"async\"\n      />\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p><em>Step 4</em>: Once we receive the LDAP Password as 'Pr1ntBind2025!', we use it to verify in NetExec\n<span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 720px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/40eb429e09ead35d175629d3d89880ff/43093/NxcResult.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 24.444444444444443%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        alt=\"NetExec Recult\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/40eb429e09ead35d175629d3d89880ff/1efcc/NxcResult.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/40eb429e09ead35d175629d3d89880ff/4abca/NxcResult.png 180w,\n/static/40eb429e09ead35d175629d3d89880ff/65e49/NxcResult.png 360w,\n/static/40eb429e09ead35d175629d3d89880ff/1efcc/NxcResult.png 720w,\n/static/40eb429e09ead35d175629d3d89880ff/43093/NxcResult.png 901w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 720px\"\n        style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n        loading=\"lazy\"\n        decoding=\"async\"\n      />\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p>[-] indicates that the account has been disabled.\nHence, we move to the next type of Coercion attack, File-Based Coercion.</p>\n<h3>File Based Coercion Attack</h3>","timeToRead":3,"frontmatter":{"title":"Active Directory Breaching","date":"June 30, 2026","description":"Breaching Active Directory. THM!"}}},"pageContext":{"slug":"/AD-Breaching/"}},"staticQueryHashes":[],"slicesMap":{}}